WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
MARK MICHANDANI: Teaching
people about cloud architecture

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.152
requires some
architecting in itself.

00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:06.360
Join us as we talk to Arctiq
about how they build out

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.485
dynamic classrooms on this
episode of "Stack Chat."

00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:10.388
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.802 --> 00:00:13.260
Tim, thanks so much
for joining us.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.550
Tell us a little bit more
about what Arctiq does.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.810
TIM FAIRWEATHER: Yeah,
so thanks for having me.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.010
Arctiq is a services
enablement firm.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.690
And so we focus on
consulting specifically

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.400
in the cloud in
the software space,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.900
a heavy focus on automation,
Kubernetes, GCP platform,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.290
and kind of on-ramping
customers and enabling

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:36.735
to do things faster, smoother,
with more efficiency.

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:38.360
MARK MICHANDANI: And
a big part of that

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.010
is also helping
them run workshops

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.793
for their own forms of training.

00:00:41.793 --> 00:00:42.710
TIM FAIRWEATHER: Yeah.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.510
So we're big
believers on enabling.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.940
We're big believers on
come in and show somebody

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.860
how to do something,
show someone how to fish.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:49.160
Right?

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.118
Teach someone how to
fish, and they can kind of

00:00:51.118 --> 00:00:52.580
be self-sustained.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.260
So the idea is these
workshops enable

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.270
us to get our
customers to be very

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.665
comfortable with the technology
and get them to touch

00:00:58.665 --> 00:01:00.290
the technology that
is ultimately going

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.010
to shape how they move forward.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:02.780
MARK MICHANDANI: So
what's an example

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.238
of what a workshop might look
like that an instructor would

00:01:05.238 --> 00:01:06.180
put together?

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.430
TIM FAIRWEATHER: Yeah, so
an example, a great example,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.650
is out GKE workshops.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.690
And GKE workshops have
been very successful for us

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.380
in just getting customers
familiar with all the concepts

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.738
inside a GKE, how to get
workloads into there,

00:01:17.738 --> 00:01:19.530
and how to manage it,
and day-two operation

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.310
sort of stuff.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.770
And ultimately, it turns
out like a shared repository

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.680
and a shared project
structure inside of GCP

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.193
that houses what we call
our instructor environment.

00:01:30.193 --> 00:01:31.610
In that instructor
environment, we

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.490
onboard instructors into there.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.960
We onboard them into
our Git repository.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.800
And effectively, they come
prepared with a student list.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.610
And all it takes really
to run a workshop

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.760
is a student list and
some students, obviously.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.970
And we get them to build
out a student list,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.010
pass it into Ansible.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.100
Ansible, ultimately, takes care
of the templating function,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.230
taking that student
list, and effectively

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:55.700
building up Terraform plans.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.700
Each student ends up with
their own environment.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.680
So part of our design
goal was making sure

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.540
that as students were
onboarded and as we

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.123
were running the
workshops, we weren't

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:07.160
running into the traditional
sort of student 4 accidentally

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.780
logged into student 1's
environment, as an example,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.020
and you end up with
that collision stuff.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.270
And very much, we
wanted to provide

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.760
an experience to our end
users that allowed them

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.010
to have their own environment.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.120
If they want to break
it, they break it.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.460
And if they want to test
the boundaries of something

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.410
that we don't have
prescribed in a lab,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.578
then we want to enable
them to do that.

00:02:26.578 --> 00:02:29.120
We want to enable them to sort
of find the edges on their own

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.943
as well.

00:02:29.943 --> 00:02:32.360
MARK MICHANDANI: So it's a big
balance between flexibility

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.570
and also being able to really
quickly get these things going

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:36.290
up and running.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.000
Sometimes you might have
students that don't show up

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.060
or changes that need to
be made, and the system

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.600
is designed to
support all of that.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:41.930
TIM FAIRWEATHER: Yes.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.430
Absolutely.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.570
So part of what we
wanted to do upfront

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.750
was give a repeatable
structure for automation,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.360
and a repeatable structure to
offer these workshops in a very

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.060
streamlined way, but then
also give the capability so

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.842
that if you're running a lab
with us and, all of a sudden,

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.800
you tear down your cluster,
which has happened,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.800
and all of a sudden
your GKE cluster's dead.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.120
Ultimately, there's
merit in troubleshooting.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.120
There's merit in kind of
going through the works

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.450
of understanding how GKE works
and how to maybe respin up

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.470
a new cluster.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.360
At the same time,
it's a workshop,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.310
and we need to sometimes get
to end of job really quickly.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.090
And so it makes more
sense to rebuild your lab.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.950
That whole structure
allows us to target

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.680
only your lab resources,
as an example,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.670
and effectively just respin
and let you get on your way.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.170
MARK MICHANDANI: What does an
example student infrastructure

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:27.680
look like?

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.640
TIM FAIRWEATHER: Mhm.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.140
Yeah, so the student
infrastructure,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.940
sort of similar
to the instructor

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.310
environment in that
there's a GKE cluster

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:35.720
inside of an ephemeral project.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.150
So the real difference
is each and every one

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.010
of the student projects is
an ephemeral project itself.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.010
And then we would onboard
each of those students

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.200
through the automation
with the right permissions

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.290
inside of that project.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.165
So they've got the right
cluster permissions,

00:03:48.165 --> 00:03:49.170
all that sort of stuff.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.795
And then, ultimately, inside of
each one of those environments,

00:03:51.795 --> 00:03:54.155
they have their own GKE
cluster with Istio enabled

00:03:54.155 --> 00:03:55.280
and a bunch of the tooling.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.655
And so we run them
through things

00:03:56.655 --> 00:03:59.690
like Cloud Build, and
GCR, and image scanning,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.750
and all that stuff all at the
project level and very much

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.450
give them their own customized
and unique environment.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.970
By controlling it all tightly
with the lifecycle management

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.520
inside of Terraform
allows us to make sure

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.670
that we can spin it up
quickly, modify and manage it

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.260
as things break or as
things need to change,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.160
but then also very much
be able to tear it down

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.493
with very little effort.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.160
MARK MICHANDANI: So
with the instructors

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.410
being able to come in there,
kind of configure everything,

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.702
and then having all the
automation come in and complete

00:04:26.702 --> 00:04:29.180
that actual running, this is
kind of a naturally volatile

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:29.690
environment.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:30.060
Right?

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.185
You're spinning things up
and spinning things down.

00:04:32.185 --> 00:04:34.730
I'm sure this wasn't
just a build it once

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.358
and it worked kind of solution.

00:04:36.358 --> 00:04:38.150
What would you say, as
you were tweaking it

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.490
a long time, especially
the automation portion,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.865
what would you say kind of
the biggest lesson you learned

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:45.050
or the biggest kind of
hurdle you overcame?

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.990
TIM FAIRWEATHER: The idea behind
it is we very quickly settled

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.540
on Terraform to handle
the actual resource

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.670
lifecycle stuff.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.040
The idea of putting
this all in code

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.890
and managing it that way was
obviously a very early design

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:58.640
goal.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.530
Part of what we had
tried to do very early on

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:04.122
was use Terraform for taking
that student list in and really

00:05:04.122 --> 00:05:05.330
building out the environment.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.965
That way, inside of Terraform
and running things on a loop,

00:05:07.965 --> 00:05:09.590
and if we had 20
students, well, that's

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.000
20 projects, 20 clusters, and
it should be very repeatable.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.840
So even enabling service
APIs at the project level

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.200
becomes a pretty significant
exercise at scale.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.210
It's one thing to
start up a project,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.450
and start up a GKE cluster,
and plumb the various elements.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.820
But we're talking about
doing it 20 times if you've

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.830
got a 20-student class.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.420
Ultimately, what
that turned into

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.340
is a little bit of a
nightmare around that.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.200
Well, what happens
if somebody's sick,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.670
and they call in before
the workshop starts,

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:40.530
and we need to substitute
person A for person B?

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.110
And ultimately, that turned
into a bit of a nightmare

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.170
very early on.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.090
And it allowed us--

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.552
or it showcased to us that we
really needed to rethink that.

00:05:48.552 --> 00:05:50.010
And so I'd say the
biggest learning

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.250
lesson was our
integrating Ansible

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:53.250
into the whole scenario.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.440
So not only is the resource
structure and the project

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.980
structure very ephemeral,
but the actual Terraform code

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.392
itself is very ephemeral.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.600
So that was probably the
biggest thing we learned out

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.140
of this whole
thing was sometimes

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.310
you've got to pick the
right tool for the job.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:07.320
MARK MICHANDANI: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.700
Thanks so much for
coming in and telling us

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.275
a little bit about how you
built this architecture.

00:06:10.275 --> 00:06:11.442
TIM FAIRWEATHER: No worries.

00:06:11.442 --> 00:06:12.317
Thanks for having me.

00:06:12.317 --> 00:06:14.025
MARK MICHANDANI:
Interested in trying out

00:06:14.025 --> 00:06:15.360
Terraform for yourself?

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.600
Check out the collection
of GCP Terraform examples

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.292
to get started.

00:06:20.292 --> 00:06:22.250
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.410
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.250
We'll see you next
time on "Stack Chat."

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

